{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Logarithmic Time Complexity.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Logarithmic Time Complexity.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"logarithmic time complexity logarithmic time complexity","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Logarithmic Time Complexity.md","fileName":"Logarithmic Time Complexity.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Logarithmic Time Complexity","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 00:13\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Algorithm Complexity Analysis]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Algorithm Complexity Analysis","Binary Search","Constant Time Complexity","algorithms.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Logarithmic Time Complexity.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Logarithmic Time Complexity.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"logarithmic time complexity logarithmic time complexity","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Logarithmic Time Complexity.md","fileName":"Logarithmic Time Complexity.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Logarithmic Time Complexity","heading":"Logarithmic Time Complexity","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Logarithmic time complexity, commonly written $O(\\log n)$, occurs when each step reduces the remaining problem by a constant factor. Repeatedly halving a search space produces a base-two logarithm.\n\nThis growth is very slow: [[Binary Search]] can locate an item among roughly one billion ordered items in about thirty probes. Logarithmic algorithms are therefore close to [[Constant Time Complexity]] in practical scalability.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Algorithm Complexity Analysis","Binary Search","Constant Time Complexity","algorithms.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Logarithmic Time Complexity.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Log